%I A401245 #27 Nov 12 2026 11:19:26
%S A401245 81119,11214431,11214491,11214719,11214727,11215177,11215213,11215229,
%T A401245 11215381,11215411,11215619,11215627,11215639,11215649,11215693,
%U A401245 11215717,11215739,11215753,11215759,11215781,11215823,11215829,11215889,11216087,11216123,11216449,11216453
%N A401245 Primes p for which pi_{24;11,13}(p) - pi_{24;1,23}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A401245 From _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_: (Start)
%C A401245 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,23} and S = {11,13}, corresponding to T = {chi_8, chi_24}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A401245 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_8(q) + chi_24(q))/2 = 1.
%C A401245 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;11,13}(x) > pi_{24;1,23}(x) has logarithmic density 0.98965, making the race between {1,23} and {11,13} the 13th most biased among the 21 races of cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24" below.
%C A401245 Let D(x) = pi_{24;11,13}(x) - pi_{24;1,23}(x). Below x0 = 7*10^12 there are respectively 30659637567, 30659688364, 30659698140, 30659716342, 30659718072, 30659718286, 30659752652, and 30659759379 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 37468. There are 10 sign-changing zones giving a total of 155257 terms below x0:
%C A401245   (1) a(1) = 81119;
%C A401245   (2) a(2..559) = 11214431-11659343 (there are 16497 primes a(2) <= p <= a(559) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -39 at p = 11621039);
%C A401245   (3) a(560..655) = 45927433-45944749 (there are 556 primes a(560) <= p <= a(655) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -19 at p = 45932087);
%C A401245   (4) a(656..748) = 53012833-53292959 (there are 2380 primes a(656) <= p <= a(748) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -29 at p = 53261927);
%C A401245   (5) a(749..2570) = 590640313-594503023 (there are 37844 primes a(749) <= p <= a(2570) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -62 at p = 593726281);
%C A401245   (6) a(2571..3504) = 681512833-685540711 (there are 135018 primes a(2571) <= p <= a(3504) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -144 at p = 684522961);
%C A401245   (7) a(3505..5779) = 7821738719-7825983409 (there are 55489 primes a(3505) <= p <= a(5779) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -59 at p = 7825391449);
%C A401245   (8) a(5780..12190) = 67084893719-67467977399 (there are 7377328 primes a(5780) <= p <= a(12190) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -1727 at p = 67162039943);
%C A401245   (9) a(12191..145075) = 5258757928103-5309747192147 (there are 133801341 primes a(12191) <= p <= a(145075) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -3374 at p = 5285320696153);
%C A401245   (10) a(145076..155257) = 5698957957487-5699624176123 (there are 2399853 primes a(145076) <= p <= a(155257) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -792 at p = 5699601967249).
%C A401245 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 143826307. (End)
%H A401245 Jianing Song, <a href="/A401245/b401245.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..12190</a> (the first 8 sign-changing zones).
%H A401245 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A401245 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24159887">Sign-changing zones of prime number races between cosets modulo 24, and of pi(x;8,5) - pi(x;8,1), up to 7*10^12</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A401245 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A401245/a401245.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..155257</a> (all terms below 7*10^12).
%H A401245 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A401245 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A401245 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A401245 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A401245 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==23, k--); if(p%24==11||p%24==13, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A401245 See A401958 for related sequences.
%K A401245 nonn,changed
%O A401245 1,1
%A A401245 _Jianing Song_, Oct 18 2026
